\import{macros}
\import{lean-macros}
\meta{agent-authored}{true}
\tag{frap}
\taxon{Theorem}
\title{Abelianizations of the local alternating groups}

\p{Let #{A_d} be the alternating group on #{d} letters, with
composition rightmost first. Then}
##{(A_3)_{\mathrm{ab}}\cong C_3,\qquad
   (A_4)_{\mathrm{ab}}\cong C_3,\qquad
   (A_d)_{\mathrm{ab}}=1\quad(d\geq5).}
\p{The corresponding commutator subgroups are stated after
Proposition 4.1 in \citet{Section 4.1, p. 11}{marchetti2025rubik}.
We give the elementary calculation, using
#{[g,h]=ghg^{-1}h^{-1}}.}

\proof{
  \p{The group #{A_3} is cyclic of order three, so already abelian.
  In #{A_4}, let #{V=\{1,(12)(34),(13)(24),(14)(23)\}}.
  Products and inverses stay in this four-element set, and conjugation
  permutes its three nonidentity elements. Hence #{V} is normal.
  The quotient has order three, so it is cyclic and
  #{[A_4,A_4]\subseteq V}. Direct multiplication gives
  #{[(123),(124)]=(12)(34)}. Conjugating this element by the
  three-cycle #{(123)} gives the other two double transpositions.
  The commutator subgroup is normal, so it contains all of #{V};
  equality follows.}
  \p{For #{d\geq5}, put #{g=(12)(34)} and #{h=(12)(35)} in #{A_d}.
  Multiplication gives #{[g,h]=(345)}. Every three-cycle is conjugate
  to this one within #{A_d}: first choose a conjugating permutation
  in #{S_d}; if it is odd, compose on the right with a transposition
  of two symbols outside #{\{3,4,5\}}. This centralizes #{(345)}
  and changes the parity. Thus the normal subgroup #{[A_d,A_d]}
  contains every three-cycle.}
  \p{Three-cycles generate all even permutations. Write an even
  permutation as a product of an even number of transpositions and
  pair them. Equal transpositions cancel; two sharing one symbol give
  a three-cycle; a disjoint pair satisfies
  #{(ab)(cd)=(acb)(acd)}. Hence every pair is a product of three-cycles,
  proving #{[A_d,A_d]=A_d}.}
}

\p{The three cases have separate formalized ingredients.
\leanref{alternatingGroup.isCyclic_of_card_le_three} gives the
cyclicity of #{A_3}. For four letters,
\leanref{alternatingGroup.kleinFour_eq_commutator} identifies #{V}
with the commutator subgroup, and
\leanref/tauceti{TauCeti.card_abelianization_alternatingGroup} gives
the quotient's cardinality three; the displayed cyclic isomorphism
then uses the elementary classification of groups of prime order.
For #{d\geq5}, \leanref{commutator_alternatingGroup_eq_top} is the
perfection statement. These links concern alternating groups, not
the entire puzzle group.}
